cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use CREAMpy in published research, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "CREAMpy: Bass diffusion + DREAM closed-economy welfare model, Python implementation"
version: 1.0.0
date-released: "2026-05-11"
license: MIT
url: "https://github.com/H1nokam1Kagura/CREAMpy"
repository-code: "https://github.com/H1nokam1Kagura/CREAMpy"

authors:
  - family-names: Hausmann
    given-names: Neil

abstract: >
  CREAMpy is a Python implementation of the DREAM (Dynamic Research Evaluation
  for Management) closed-economy partial-equilibrium surplus model, combined with
  a discrete Bass diffusion adoption model. Together they form a complete,
  Excel-free pipeline for estimating the economic welfare returns to agricultural
  research investment: Bass diffusion generates the adoption schedule; the DREAM
  welfare model translates it into producer and consumer surplus NPVs.
  The implementation follows Alston, Norton & Pardey (1995) Ch. 4–5 and the
  IFPRI DREAM manual (Wood, Maredia & Pardey 2001). Both K-shift (cost reduction)
  and J-shift (yield-augmenting) supply shift conventions are supported.

references:
  - type: book
    title: "Science Under Scarcity: Principles and Practice for Agricultural Research Evaluation and Priority Setting"
    authors:
      - family-names: Alston
        given-names: Julian M.
      - family-names: Norton
        given-names: George W.
      - family-names: Pardey
        given-names: Philip G.
    year: 1995
    publisher:
      name: Cornell University Press
    notes: "Chapters 4–5 provide the closed-economy partial-equilibrium model implemented here."

  - type: report
    title: "Prioritizing agricultural research for sustainable development using DREAM version 3.0"
    authors:
      - family-names: Wood
        given-names: Stanley
      - family-names: Maredia
        given-names: Mywish
      - family-names: Pardey
        given-names: Philip G.
    year: 2001
    institution:
